1
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:06,476
The blocks live in the public bucket, one folder per day.

2
00:00:06,756 --> 00:00:11,893
We read them straight from S3, with read parquet and a wildcard: one month at a time.

3
00:00:12,843 --> 00:00:17,813
Three statements: create the table with August, then insert September, then October.

4
00:00:18,093 --> 00:00:22,250
Each one becomes a snapshot of its own, which we use later for time travel.

5
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:24,346
One setting first.

6
00:00:24,626 --> 00:00:29,135
DuckLake can keep small writes inside the catalog, which is called data inlining.

7
00:00:29,415 --> 00:00:33,066
We turn it off, so every write becomes Parquet files we can look at.

8
00:00:33,346 --> 00:00:35,198
Lesson 4.3 explains it.

9
00:00:36,148 --> 00:00:39,276
Open the SQL Editor, and pick the eth lake connection.

10
00:00:39,556 --> 00:00:41,912
Here is the script: one statement per line.

11
00:00:45,166 --> 00:00:50,640
Run the first two lines: inlining off, and a schema called raw, for the data as it comes in.

12
00:00:51,590 --> 00:00:55,289
Line three creates raw dot blocks from every file of August.

13
00:00:55,569 --> 00:00:59,726
Star exclude drops two columns we do not need, like the big logs bloom.

14
00:01:00,006 --> 00:01:04,292
Run it: Studio downloads the month from S3 and writes it into the lake.

15
00:01:20,071 --> 00:01:22,643
Then September, and October, with insert into.

16
00:02:16,357 --> 00:02:18,234
Check the result: blocks per month.

17
00:02:18,514 --> 00:02:23,059
About two hundred twenty thousand each, almost six hundred sixty thousand in total.

18
00:02:24,009 --> 00:02:26,760
Now open Data Lake, and the eth lake instance.

19
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:27,719
Refresh.

20
00:02:27,999 --> 00:02:32,346
The new table is listed: blocks, in schema raw, with its rows and size.

21
00:02:34,992 --> 00:02:39,190
And History has four new snapshots: the schema, then one for each month.

22
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:41,737
Keep the script in the project.

23
00:02:42,017 --> 00:02:50,875
In DBT Studio, add a folder called sql, and in it a file called 2 underscore 2 load blocks dot sequel, with the same statements.

24
00:02:51,155 --> 00:02:51,754
Save.

25
00:02:56,501 --> 00:03:01,272
Git tab: stage all, commit with load three months of blocks, and Sync Changes to push.

26
00:03:01,552 --> 00:03:05,148
The lake itself stays out of git; the script is enough to rebuild it.

27
00:03:14,141 --> 00:03:21,655
To recap: we turned inlining off, made the raw schema, and loaded three months of blocks from S3, one month per statement.

28
00:03:21,935 --> 00:03:25,816
That is six hundred sixty thousand blocks, and four new snapshots.

29
00:03:26,097 --> 00:03:28,963
Next, we look inside the lake: the files on disk.
